| Type                          | Use Case                |
| ----------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| `List<List<Integer>>`         | simple unweighted graph |
| `List<List<Edge>>`            | weighted graph          |
| `ArrayList<Integer>[]`        | fast CP unweighted      |
| `ArrayList<Edge>[]`           | Dijkstra / MST          |
| `Map<Integer, List<Integer>>` | sparse / non-continuous |
| `Map<String, List<String>>`   | real-world graphs       |
| `Node class`                  | OOP / system design     |


DECISION TREE OF WHEN TO USE WHAT

Start
 │
 ├── Nodes are integers?
 │       │
 │       ├── Yes
 │       │     ├── Weighted?
 │       │     │       ├── Yes → ArrayList<Edge>[] OR List<List<Edge>>
 │       │     │       └── No  → List<List<Integer>> OR ArrayList<Integer>[]
 │       │
 │       └── No (String/Object)
 │             ├── Weighted?
 │             │       ├── Yes → Map<K, List<Edge>>
 │             │       └── No  → Map<K, List<K>>
 │             │
 │             └── Need no duplicates?
 │                     └── Use Set instead of List
 │
 └── End




Here are the **standard adjacency list graph templates in Java**—the ones you’ll actually use in interviews and competitive programming.

I’ll group them cleanly so you can remember them easily.

---

# 🧠 1. `List<List<Integer>>` (most common CP template)

### ✔️ Best for: integer nodes (0 to n-1)

```java id="t1"
int n = 5;
List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
}
```

### ➕ Add edge

```java id="t2"
graph.get(u).add(v);
graph.get(v).add(u); // undirected
```

---

# 🧠 2. `List<List<Edge>>` (weighted graph)

### ✔️ Best for: weighted graphs

```java id="t3"
class Edge {
    int v, w;
    Edge(int v, int w) {
        this.v = v;
        this.w = w;
    }
}
```

### Graph:

```java id="t4"
int n = 5;
List<List<Edge>> graph = new ArrayList<>();

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
}
```

### Add edge:

```java id="t5"
graph.get(u).add(new Edge(v, w));
graph.get(v).add(new Edge(u, w)); // undirected
```

---

# 🧠 3. `ArrayList<Integer>[]` (array of lists)

### ✔️ Fast CP-style alternative

```java id="t6"
int n = 5;
ArrayList<Integer>[] graph = new ArrayList[n];

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    graph[i] = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

### Add edge:

```java id="t7"
graph[u].add(v);
graph[v].add(u);
```

---

# 🧠 4. `ArrayList<Edge>[]` (classic weighted CP)

### ✔️ Most used in Dijkstra / shortest path

```java id="t8"
class Edge {
    int v, w;
    Edge(int v, int w) {
        this.v = v;
        this.w = w;
    }
}

int n = 5;
ArrayList<Edge>[] graph = new ArrayList[n];

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    graph[i] = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

### Add edge:

```java id="t9"
graph[u].add(new Edge(v, w));
graph[v].add(new Edge(u, w));
```

---

# 🧠 5. `Map<Integer, List<Integer>>` (flexible graph)

### ✔️ When nodes are not continuous

```java id="t10"
Map<Integer, List<Integer>> graph = new HashMap<>();
```

### Add edge:

```java id="t11"
graph.computeIfAbsent(u, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(v);
graph.computeIfAbsent(v, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(u);
```

---

# 🧠 6. `Map<String, List<String>>` (real-world graphs)

### ✔️ Used in LC 126, word problems, dependency graphs

```java id="t12"
Map<String, List<String>> graph = new HashMap<>();
```

### Add edge:

```java id="t13"
graph.computeIfAbsent(u, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(v);
```

---

# 🧠 7. `Map<String, Set<String>>` (no duplicates)

```java id="t14"
Map<String, Set<String>> graph = new HashMap<>();
```

### Add edge:

```java id="t15"
graph.computeIfAbsent(u, k -> new HashSet<>()).add(v);
```

---

# 🧠 8. OOP-style Node graph (advanced)

```java id="t16"
class Node {
    int val;
    List<Node> neighbors = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

### Use case:

* Trees
* Design problems
* Object-based graphs

